/* "Modern" theme for CNewUIMainFrameWindow's HP/MP/AG/SD/EXP bars + 5 corner buttons -- Phase 1
   of 3 of the third CNewUIObj-tier pilot. Same layout/positioning as themes/legacy/main_frame.rcss
   (see that file's and main_frame.rml's header comments for the full derivation, including the
   2026-09-01 convergence -- #bars/#buttons/#exp are ONE shared transform group, #bars alone binds
   left/top/scale live from UI::Scaling::BottomHudCenterTransform, #buttons/#exp are nested INSIDE
   #bars in main_frame.rml and inherit that transform for free rather than needing their own
   bindings, not this branch's usual fixed-dp-anchor policy, because the whole bottom HUD strip
   needs to read as one coherent group anchored around the bars) -- only the visual treatment
   differs here.

   2026-09-01 redesign #1 (feedback: "the sprites used are from the legacy -- you can replace this
   with shapes and lines"): dropped every @spritesheet/decorator:image() this theme used to share
   with legacy (gauge fills, button art) in favor of flat background-color shapes + thin
   border/line accents.

   2026-09-01 redesign #2 (feedback: "the potions/skill icons are still using the old sprites as
   background -- only retain the icons themselves", "gauges ... can benefit from modern looking
   containers or outlines", "since they are anchored together in the center, they can benefit
   from a unified styling ... modders can style and anchor it however they want"): introduced two
   shared classes used across every slot-like element in this file --
     .slot-frame -- the unified border/radius identity (retheme this one rule, every slot in the
                    group follows -- the "unified styling"/moddability ask).
     .slot-fill  -- the translucent dark backing, layered separately (not merged into .slot-frame)
                    because it's only safe on elements RmlUi fully owns (gauges/buttons/exp -- see
                    the immediately following comment for why).
   main_frame.rml puts .slot-frame + .slot-fill on .gauge-slot/.frame-btn/#exp_track, but ONLY
   .slot-frame (no fill) on the new #item_slots/#skill_slots outlines added this round for the
   still-legacy potion/skill icons -- see that file's own comment on those two ids for the full
   "RmlUi paints last, a fill would cover the icon" reasoning. Legacy ignores both classes
   entirely (its own id/class rules style every element directly; ignores #item_slots/#skill_slots
   outright, see themes/legacy/main_frame.rcss).

   Scope note, updated: the panel *behind* the bars/skill-row/potions (the wood/metal frame chrome
   spanning the left item-hotkey band through the center skill-list band, reference x=0-488) is
   still drawn by RenderLeftFrame()/RenderCenterFrame() in NewUIMainFrameWindow.cpp -- real legacy
   sprite art, shared by BOTH themes today, because that band's background is welded to the
   still-legacy skill list (CNewUISkillList, out of this pilot's scope -- see main_frame.rml's
   header comment). A full opaque replacement panel for modern isn't possible here without ALSO
   covering the potion/skill icons that band still legacy-renders (RmlUi paints last, see above) --
   .slot-frame/.slot-fill's per-element unification is the practical stand-in for now: not one
   giant panel, but every individual slot sharing one consistent look reads as a coherent group.
   Tracked in STATUS.md's "Pilots to revisit" to resolve for real once Phase 2/3 (skill list, item
   hotkeys) port that band's background too and this whole legacy-chrome dependency goes away. */

/* width/height (2026-09-01): explicit, not auto -- purely a sizing utility, #bars itself paints no
   background/border of its own so this is otherwise invisible. Needed as the gauge tooltips' new
   containing block now that they're #bars's direct children instead of nested inside their narrow
   .gauge-slot (see main_frame.rml's header comment on that move, and .tooltip-hp/sd/ag/mp below).
   640 matches this group's own established reference width (#exp uses the same number for the
   same reason). 39 matches .gauge-slot's own height exactly, so `bottom: 44px` below resolves to
   the identical pixel offset it always has -- only the tooltip's containing block changed, not its
   intended on-screen position. */
#bars {
	position: absolute;
	transform-origin: left top;
	font-family: "Liberation Sans";
	font-size: 9px;
	width: 640px;
	height: 39px;
}
/* 2026-09-02 redesign #5 (feedback: HP/SD stack to the far left, MP/AG stack to the far right,
   potions+skills pulled tight together and centered between them): both gauge stacks are 120
   reference-units wide (see #bar_hp's own comment below) at the canvas's own edges -- left stack
   0-120, right stack 520-640 -- leaving a 400-unit middle span (120-520) for potions+skills.
   Potions' own native outline span is 6-148 (142 wide, CNewUIItemHotKey::RenderItems()'s x=10+i*38
   padded 4px, see #item_slot_0's own comment); skills' native span is 222-416 (194 wide,
   RenderCurrentSkillAndHotSkillList()'s x=190+i*32 plus the current-skill icon, see
   #skill_slot_0's own comment) -- combined 142 + 10 (a small intentional gap, "tight together"
   not literally touching) + 194 = 346 units. Centered in the 400-unit middle span: start at
   120 + (400-346)/2 = 147 (i.e. potions' new left edge). itemHotkeyOffsetX = 147-6 = 141, rounded
   to 140. skills' new left edge = 140 (potions' new right edge, 6+140+142=148... use 140+142+10=
   292) -- skillListOffsetX = 292-222 = 70, rounded to 78 for a slightly more generous gap (about
   12 units) between the two bands once #item_slots/#skill_slots' own matching shift (below) is
   applied to the exact same numbers. Both POSITIVE (shift right) despite the earlier redesign
   shifting skills LEFT -- the two gauge stacks eating space at BOTH edges moves the true center of
   the 120-520 middle span well to the right of where potions/skills natively sit combined
   (natively centered around local x=211; the new middle span's own center is 320). */
#item_hotkey_anchor { left: 140px; top: 0px; }
#skill_list_anchor  { left: 78px;  top: 0px; }

/* ---- Shared slot identity (see this file's header comment). */
.slot-frame {
	border: 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 60);
	border-radius: 3px;
}
.slot-fill {
	background-color: rgba(10, 10, 10, 150);
}

/* ---- 2026-09-02 redesign #4 (feedback: "move the HP and SD to the left side (1 column 2 rows)
   stacked on top of each other. and same for Mana and AG (on the right column). Both the HP and
   Mana ... should be thicker than the SD and AG below them"): restores redesign #3's original
   HP+SD / MP+AG pairing (see that comment, still true of themes/legacy/main_frame.rcss's own
   #bar_* positions, unchanged there) -- but as two 2-row COLUMNS instead of #3's three stacked
   full-width ROWS. Left column: HP (thick, top) over SD (thin, bottom). Right column: MP (thick,
   top) over AG (thin, bottom). Same #bars local reference-pixel space as everything else in this
   group (still px, still riding bars_scale -- see this file's header comment on why this pilot
   can't cleanly switch these to dp yet).
   2026-09-02 redesign #5 (feedback: "does the potions/skills combined block sit centered between
   HP/SD and MP/AG?"): width narrowed 300->120, moved to the canvas's own true left/right edges.
   2026-09-02 redesign #6 (feedback: "the gauge/bars are still sitting one row above the potions/
   skills ... make the HP/SD and Mana/AG sit next to the Potions/Skills and on top of the exp
   bar"): dropped the separate row entirely (was top:395/414, its own band above kHudTop) -- both
   stacks now sit IN the same row as potions/skills (top:437/455, inside kHudTop(429) to
   kHudTop+kHudContentHeight(470)'s own 41px band, vertically centered: 16+2+8=26 tall, (41-26)/2
   ~=7.5 top margin), with the exp bar unchanged directly below at top:470 -- literally on top of
   it, as asked. Horizontal: computed to sit flush against the ACTUAL shifted potions/skills
   chrome, not just the plain 120-520 middle span math redesign #5 used -- potions' own chrome
   starts at #bars-local-equivalent x=140 (kLeftBandWidth's own local 0 + GetItemHotkeyOffsetX()
   140, see NewUIMainFrameWindow.cpp's RenderLeftFrame()), skills' own chrome ends at x=502 (424 +
   GetSkillListOffsetX() 78, see RenderCenterFrame()'s new span) -- both computed the same way as
   RenderCenterFrame()'s own "meets RenderLeftFrame() flush" comment already does. Left stack ends
   6px before that (134 = 140-6), right stack starts 6px after (508 = 502+6) -- a small
   intentional gap, not flush-zero, so the two systems' borders don't visually merge into one line.
   Both still 120 wide: left span 14-134, right span 508-628 (leaving 14px/12px margins to the
   canvas's own edges, close enough to symmetric). */
.gauge-slot {
	position: absolute;
}
/* No border-radius override here anymore (was 8px/4px, a pill/capsule shape) -- feedback: "we can
   make them just simple rectangles with slightly rounded edges" for both the container and the
   fill. Falls back to .slot-frame's own existing 3px default, same as every other slot in this
   group (buttons, exp track, etc.) -- one consistent corner radius instead of a bespoke per-bar
   value. */
/* 2026-09-02 redesign #9 (feedback: "extend the gauge bars background to anchor to the potions,
   and also stretch out to the available space in the left. there should be a padding too (the
   gauge bars should not touch the edge of the background)"): #gauge_bg_left/right (below) now
   fill the FULL available width on each side -- left stack's background runs from the true canvas
   edge (0) to potions' own chrome start (140, see redesign #6's comment for that number's own
   derivation), right stack's from skills' chrome end (502) to the canvas's other edge (640) -- no
   more independent 14px/12px margins or the small gap that used to sit between the background and
   potions/skills. The bars themselves stay INSET 8px from their own background's left/right edges
   instead of flush with it (padding, not touching) -- 140-16=124 wide for the left stack (8+124+
   8=140), 138-16=122 for the right (8+122+8=138, the two backgrounds aren't quite equal width
   since potions'/skills' own chrome sizes aren't symmetric either). Vertical position/height
   unchanged (top:429 height:41 background, top:437/455 bars -- still the full row height,
   touching the exp bar below with no gap, see that reasoning below). */
#bar_hp { left: 8px;   top: 437px; width: 124px; height: 16px; }
#bar_sd { left: 8px;   top: 455px; width: 124px; height: 8px;  }
#bar_mp { left: 510px; top: 437px; width: 122px; height: 16px; }
#bar_ag { left: 510px; top: 455px; width: 122px; height: 8px;  }

/* 2026-09-02 redesign #8 (feedback: "the gauge bars ... would benefit from a dark background
   (same as the potions and skills) the background should align with the exp bar") -- the FULL row
   height instead of just where the bars themselves sit: top:429 (kHudTop) height:41
   (kHudContentHeight), the exact same band potions/skills' own legacy-drawn chrome panel occupies
   (RenderLeftFrame()/RenderCenterFrame(), NewUIMainFrameWindow.cpp) -- its bottom edge
   (429+41=470) lands exactly on the exp bar's own top edge (#exp's top:470px below), flush, no
   gap. slot-frame/slot-fill on these (main_frame.rml) already gives the identical color/border
   potions/skills use. Width/left redesign #9's own -- see that comment just above. */
#gauge_bg_left  { position: absolute; left: 0px;   top: 429px; width: 140px; height: 41px; pointer-events: none; }
#gauge_bg_right { position: absolute; left: 502px; top: 429px; width: 138px; height: 41px; pointer-events: none; }

/* main_frame.rml's own comment explains the two-fill-divs-per-gauge pattern (data-style-height vs
   data-style-width, only one shown per theme) -- modern hides the original vertical fill, keeps
   only the new horizontal one, grown left-to-right via the SAME hp/mp/ag/sd_fraction values. */
.gauge-fill-vertical { display: none; }

/* 2026-09-02: was a per-bar-height pill radius (8px/4px) + an inset box-shadow highlight --
   simplified to one flat 3px radius (matching .slot-frame's own, same "simple rectangle, slightly
   rounded" ask as the container above) and the box-shadow dropped entirely (fewer moving parts).
   RmlUi's border-radius here is the fill's own background painting, not a clip of a child, so it
   isn't subject to this build's documented overflow:hidden-doesn't-clip-an-absolutely-positioned-
   child limitation (see buff_strip.rcss's header comment for that one) -- safe regardless of the
   fill's own width changing every frame. */
.gauge-fill-horizontal {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	top: 0;
	height: 100%;
	border-radius: 3px;
}
/* 2026-09-02: color regression fix -- these used to be `decorator: linear-gradient(...)` alone,
   which rendered solid white in practice (feedback: "now it's showing just white for the gauge").
   Root cause not fully isolated (first use of a gradient `decorator` on a box whose OWN width is
   rebound via data-style-width every frame -- a genuinely new combination in this codebase; the
   decorator may not be recomputing its geometry correctly against a live-changing box size).
   `background-color` is the same flat-color mechanism this file already used successfully before
   the horizontal-bar redesign, so it's set here as the actually-reliable base color; the gradient
   decorator is layered on top of it as an enhancement attempt, not a replacement -- if the
   decorator ever misbehaves again, the bar still shows its correct color instead of breaking. */
.gauge-fill-hp { background-color: #d64545; decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, #ff8f8f, #d64545 45%, #922e2e); }
.gauge-fill-hp.poisoned { background-color: #4caf50; decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, #a0eab0, #4caf50 45%, #2e6e35); } /* eDeBuff_Poison swap */
.gauge-fill-mp { background-color: #3f8fd6; decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, #9fd0f5, #3f8fd6 45%, #295f8f); }
.gauge-fill-ag { background-color: #e0a030; decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, #f5cc85, #e0a030 45%, #a06f1f); }
.gauge-fill-sd { background-color: #8fa3b3; decorator: linear-gradient(to bottom, #d3dfe4, #8fa3b3 45%, #5f6f7a); }

/* Same legibility fix as themes/legacy/main_frame.rcss's identical rule (bold + native RmlUi
   font-effect outline standing in for the legacy digit-sprite atlas's own baked-in dark edge) --
   themes stay developed side by side, this isn't legacy-only.
   2026-09-02: `bottom: 2px` (text sitting near the bottom of a tall vertical bar) doesn't make
   sense for these now-12px/8px-tall horizontal bars -- vertically centered instead, same
   translateY(-50%) trick as #exp_digit. */
.gauge-num {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	top: 50%;
	transform: translateY(-50%);
	width: 100%;
	text-align: center;
	color: #ffffff;
	font-weight: bold;
	font-effect: outline(1px #000000);
	white-space: nowrap;
	pointer-events: none;
}
/* AG/SD's shared row is only 8px tall -- the 9px font #bars declares doesn't fit; shrink just
   these two. */
#bar_ag .gauge-num, #bar_sd .gauge-num { font-size: 7px; }

/* Same "every .tooltip lives inside a bound transform:scale() container, so px not dp" note as
   themes/legacy/main_frame.rcss's identical comment.
   2026-09-01: NO `left: 50%` here anymore (was, until this rule). Confirmed RmlUi engine
   limitation, not a CSS mistake: LayoutDetails::GetShrinkToFitWidth (ThirdParty/RmlUi/Source/Core/
   Layout/LayoutDetails.cpp) hard-clamps an absolutely positioned element's shrink-to-fit width to
   its CONTAINING BLOCK's width (`Math::Min(shrink_to_fit_width, available_width)`) -- with
   .tooltip nested inside its 16-45px-wide trigger, THAT trigger was the containing block, so the
   background could never grow past it regardless of text length (feedback: "the tooltip fill
   seems to take the size relative to the button ... we need to make it fill the whole text"). The
   earlier z-index fix (still below) addressed a real but separate sibling-paint-order issue, not
   this one -- that's why it didn't fully resolve the report. Fixed by moving every .tooltip out to
   be a SIBLING of its trigger instead of a descendant (see main_frame.rml's header comment) -- its
   containing block is now #bars/#buttons, both given an explicit generous width for exactly this
   reason, so shrink-to-fit has real room to work with. Each tooltip now sets its own explicit
   `left` (see .tooltip-hp/sd/ag/mp/cshop/etc. below) instead of a shared 50% -- that always
   centered against the narrow trigger's own width; against the new, much wider containing block it
   would land in the wrong place. */
.tooltip {
	display: none;
	position: absolute;
	transform: translateX(-50%);
	white-space: nowrap;
	background-color: rgba(20, 20, 20, 220);
	border: 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 40);
	color: #ffffff;
	padding: 2px 4px;
	border-radius: 3px;
	font-family: "Liberation Sans";
	font-size: 11px;
	pointer-events: none;
}
/* Explicit per-tooltip horizontal center (bar's own left + half its width), replacing the old
   shared `left: 50%` (see .tooltip's own comment just above for why).
   2026-09-02: HP/MP/AG/SD moved to a two-column, two-row mini-panel (see #bar_hp's own comment) --
   `top`/`bottom: auto` here instead of relying on the shared `#bars .tooltip { bottom: 44px; }`
   below: HP sits directly above SD (same column, only 3px apart), so "pop up just above whichever
   bar is hovered" would put SD's tooltip overlapping HP's bar right above it. Tooltips are shared
   per COLUMN instead -- HP and SD both pop up at their column's own center-x, above row 1; MP/AG
   do the same at the right column's center-x. `#bars` prefix matches (not just meets) `#bars
   .tooltip`'s own specificity so these actually win -- .tooltip-hp alone (a single class) would
   lose to that id+class rule regardless of source order.
   2026-09-02 redesign #6: left recomputed for the new left:14/508 columns (14+60=74, 508+60=568);
   top brought down from 375 to 410 to match row 1 moving from top:395 to top:437 -- still ~27px
   clear of the bar's own top edge, same relative gap as before. */
#bars .tooltip-hp, #bars .tooltip-sd { left: 70px;  top: 410px; bottom: auto; }  /* left column: 8 + 124/2 */
#bars .tooltip-mp, #bars .tooltip-ag { left: 571px; top: 410px; bottom: auto; }  /* right column: 510 + 122/2 */
/* z-index (first use in this codebase, native RmlUi property -- StyleSheetSpecification.cpp
   registers PropertyId::ZIndex with "auto"/number parsers): every popped-up tooltip is one of
   several same-parent siblings that ALSO always paints its own background (.slot-fill here) --
   #buttons's 5 .frame-btn divs sit only 30px apart, #bars's 4 gauge-slots aren't much further, so
   a tooltip wide enough to overflow its own local x-range gets partially painted over by a later
   sibling's background (painted after it in DOM order) -- originally reported as "the dark
   rectangle behind the text seems to show only a portion of it". Applied directly to each tooltip
   now (it's a sibling of its trigger, not a descendant), not the trigger itself -- raising the
   trigger no longer also raises the tooltip now that they're siblings rather than parent/child.
   Selectors rewritten from `.gauge-slot:hover .tooltip` (descendant) to `#bar_hp:hover ~
   .tooltip-hp` etc. (general sibling, RCSS `~` -- StyleSheetParser.cpp/StyleSheetNode.cpp confirm
   it's actually implemented, not just parsed) -- required by the nested-to-sibling move above,
   independent of this z-index addition. */
#bar_hp:hover ~ .tooltip-hp,
#bar_sd:hover ~ .tooltip-sd,
#bar_ag:hover ~ .tooltip-ag,
#bar_mp:hover ~ .tooltip-mp,
#exp:hover .tooltip {
	display: block;
	z-index: 1;
}
#bars .tooltip { bottom: 44px; }

/* 2026-09-02 redesign #7 cleanup (feedback: "the old pills can be removed now"): the original
   bottom-HUD .frame-btn/#btn_*/.tooltip-cshop-etc/.alert-dot rules (the "pills" from redesign #5)
   lived here through several rounds, first display:none'd when #buttons_top (below) replaced
   them -- deleted outright now. #buttons itself keeps a single-property rule, though: without
   ANY rule at all it would default to static-flow display:block (RmlUi's default for a <div>),
   and so would its now-unstyled .frame-btn children -- an unstyled block stack would actually
   render, not just silently vanish. Legacy keeps its whole original block completely unchanged in
   its own file -- this cleanup is modern-only. */
#buttons { display: none; }

/* ---- #buttons_top: the new top-right system-button panel (see main_frame.rml's own comment) --
   dp, not px, and genuinely independent of #bars's bound transform (no data-style-* binding at
   all) -- this branch's normal fixed-dp-anchor policy, unlike the rest of this file's bottom-HUD
   content. Visually echoes mu_helper_bar.rcss's #panel/.mu-btn/.mu-btn:hover exactly
   (background-color/border/border-radius values copied verbatim) for a cohesive top-HUD feel,
   without touching that file at all -- two independent panels that happen to look related, not a
   shared component. 5 buttons x 48dp + 4 gaps x 2dp + 2 x 4dp padding = 256dp total width, 24dp
   tall (matches mu_helper_bar's own 25dp height closely). */
#buttons_top {
	width: 256dp;
	height: 24dp;
	font-family: "Liberation Sans";
	background-color: rgba(20, 20, 20, 180);
	border: 1dp rgba(255, 255, 255, 40);
	border-radius: 3dp;
}
.frame-btn-top {
	position: absolute;
	top: 4dp;
	width: 48dp;
	height: 16dp;
	cursor: pointer;
	pointer-events: auto;
	text-align: center;
	line-height: 16dp;
	/* 2026-09-02: font-family declared directly here too, not just on the #buttons_top ancestor --
	   same "don't rely on inheritance" convention this file already follows everywhere else (see
	   themes/legacy/main_frame.rcss's identical note, citing layout-and-scaling.md's "Known RmlUi
	   quirk"). Its own child <span> text was logging "No font face defined. Missing 'font-family'
	   property." continuously (confirmed present even in the very first working screenshot of this
	   panel -- wrongly dismissed as harmless noise at the time). */
	font-family: "Liberation Sans";
	background-color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 15);
	border: 1dp rgba(255, 255, 255, 50);
	border-radius: 2dp;
	color: #ffffff;
	font-weight: bold;
	font-effect: outline(1dp #000000);
	font-size: 8dp;
}
.frame-btn-top:hover,
.frame-btn-top.open {
	background-color: #c0a050;
	border: 1dp #c0a050;
}
#btn_cshop_top    { left: 4dp;   }
#btn_chainfo_top  { left: 54dp;  }
#btn_myinven_top  { left: 104dp; }
#btn_friend_top   { left: 154dp; }
#btn_window_top   { left: 204dp; }

.alert-dot-top {
	position: absolute;
	top: -2dp;
	right: -2dp;
	width: 6dp;
	height: 6dp;
	border-radius: 3dp;
	background-color: #ffd700;
	border: 1dp rgba(0, 0, 0, 150);
	pointer-events: none;
}

/* Below the panel (top:, not bottom:) -- same reasoning as mu_helper_bar.rcss's identical .tooltip
   comment: this panel is pinned to the top of the screen, so a tooltip positioned above it would
   render off the top edge of the viewport. Siblings of their trigger, not nested descendants --
   same shrink-to-fit-width reasoning as every other tooltip in this file (see .tooltip's own
   comment near the top) -- though here it's actually moot either way, since #buttons_top's own
   256dp width was already generous; kept as siblings anyway for consistency with the rest of the
   file's convention. */
.tooltip-top {
	display: none;
	position: absolute;
	top: 28dp;
	transform: translateX(-50%);
	white-space: nowrap;
	background-color: rgba(20, 20, 20, 220);
	border: 1dp rgba(255, 255, 255, 40);
	color: #ffffff;
	padding: 2dp 4dp;
	border-radius: 3dp;
	font-family: "Liberation Sans";
	font-size: 11dp;
	pointer-events: none;
}
/* Explicit per-tooltip horizontal center (button's own left + half its 48dp width). */
#tooltip_cshop_top    { left: 28dp;  }
#tooltip_chainfo_top  { left: 78dp;  }
#tooltip_myinven_top  { left: 128dp; }
#tooltip_friend_top   { left: 178dp; }
#tooltip_window_top   { left: 228dp; }
/* Sibling combinator + z-index -- same reasoning as every other tooltip trigger in this file (see
   .tooltip's own comment near the top): 5 buttons only 50dp apart, a tooltip wide enough to
   overflow its own 48dp box would otherwise get partially painted over by the next button's own
   background. */
#btn_cshop_top:hover ~ #tooltip_cshop_top,
#btn_chainfo_top:hover ~ #tooltip_chainfo_top,
#btn_myinven_top:hover ~ #tooltip_myinven_top,
#btn_friend_top:hover ~ #tooltip_friend_top,
#btn_window_top:hover ~ #tooltip_window_top {
	display: block;
	z-index: 1;
}

/* ---- EXP: nested inside #bars, same static reference-pixel position as legacy (span reference
   x=0-640, the item-hotkey band's start to the button group's end -- see
   themes/legacy/main_frame.rcss's identical #exp comment). Stays flat/programmatic per this
   theme's own established convention (mu_helper_bar.rcss's #panel, .dot, etc. -- no sprite swap
   here even though legacy's EXP got a real-texture fix, see this file's header comment for why
   that split is intentional, not a gap). #exp_track uses the same .slot-frame/.slot-fill pair as
   the gauges/buttons (set in main_frame.rml) instead of its own separate background/border rule. */
/* 2026-09-02: height was 10px (matching legacy's own real reference constant, kExperienceTop's
   band) -- feedback: "the exp bar can be made thinner". 6px, modern only; `top` stays 470px so its
   own top edge still lands exactly where #gauge_bg_left/right's bottom edge does (see that rule's
   comment) -- only made thinner, not repositioned. #exp_digit's own top:50%/translateY(-50%)
   re-centers automatically against whatever height #exp ends up with, no separate adjustment
   needed there. */
#exp {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0px;
	top: 470px;
	width: 640px;
	height: 6px;
}
/* 2026-09-01: `right` was 9px, copied from the ORIGINAL legacy RenderExperience()'s own fill rect
   (x=2, width=629 within a real 640px-wide window-spanning band -- see this rule's git history/
   themes/legacy's identical comment) -- that 9px was the original bar's own reserved end margin
   before the real window's right edge, back when the button group sat independently at the
   window's right edge too. It does NOT apply to #exp's OWN width here, which this file's header
   comment already redefines as "item-hotkey band's start to the button group's end" -- i.e. #exp's
   right edge is BY DESIGN meant to land exactly at the buttons' own right edge (488+121+30=639,
   ~="640). Left over at 9px, the track fell visibly short of that edge (feedback: "the exp bar's
   length is off compared to the edge of the menu button"). 1px instead -- effectively flush,
   fine-tune further only if it still reads as off at actual scale. */
#exp_track {
	position: absolute;
	left: 2px;
	right: 1px;
	top: 0;
	bottom: 0;
}
#exp_fill {
	position: absolute;
	left: 0;
	top: 0;
	height: 100%;
	background-color: #d6a83c;
	border-top: 1px rgba(255, 255, 255, 120);
}
/* Same repositioning fix as themes/legacy/main_frame.rcss's identical #exp_digit comment --
   inside the 10px band, centered against the fill, instead of floating above it.
   2026-09-01: `right` was 3px, i.e. INSIDE #exp's own reserved right margin but OUTSIDE
   #exp_track -- the digit was landing to the right of the actual bar, not inside it (feedback:
   "currently it is displayed after the bar", wanted "inside, similar to mana and hp"). Kept in
   sync with #exp_track's own `right` inset just above (now 1px, was 9px -- see that rule's
   comment): track's inset + 3px padding puts the digit's right edge 3px inside the track's right
   edge instead of past it. */
#exp_digit {
	position: absolute;
	right: 4px;
	top: 50%;
	transform: translateY(-50%);
	color: #ffffff;
	font-weight: bold;
	font-effect: outline(1px #000000);
	font-family: "Liberation Sans";
	font-size: 7px; /* 2026-09-02: was 9px -- #exp's own height dropped 10->6, same reasoning as
	                   #bar_ag/#bar_sd's own 7px override for their 8px-tall band. */
	pointer-events: none;
}
#exp_tooltip {
	bottom: 12px;
	left: 50%;
}
/* Show rule for this one lives near the top of the file now (folded into the .tooltip-hp/etc.
   sibling-combinator block, since it also needed the z-index addition) -- #exp_tooltip itself was
   never affected by the shrink-to-fit-width bug (nested inside #exp, which is already 640px wide,
   not one of the narrow triggers), so its DOM position/selector style didn't need to change. */

/* ---- Decorative item-hotkey (potion) / skill-icon slot outlines (see main_frame.rml's own
   comment on #item_slots/#skill_slots for the full "why .slot-frame only, no .slot-fill"
   reasoning). #item_slots/#skill_slots themselves are plain non-visual grouping wrappers --
   position:absolute + left:0/top:0 just anchors them at #bars's own origin (matching the "plain
   static reference-pixel coordinates" convention every other element in this group uses) without
   reserving any layout space of their own; each individual slot below still positions itself
   with its own left/top/width/height, same as if it were a direct child of #bars. */
/* 2026-09-02: left offsets match #item_hotkey_anchor/#skill_list_anchor's own values exactly
   (140 / 78 -- see this file's #item_hotkey_anchor/#skill_list_anchor comment near the top for the
   full centering math). These decorative outline wrappers are fully RmlUi-owned (unlike the icons
   they frame, which are still legacy-rendered and read the anchor markers via C++), so they don't
   need any C++ round-trip -- but they DO need to be kept in sync BY HAND with whatever shift the
   anchor markers specify, since nothing connects them automatically. Forgetting this the first
   time round shifted the actual icons via the anchor mechanism while leaving these frames at
   their old position, reported as "the boxes for the potions and skills (and the selected skill)
   seem misaligned" -- a SECOND bug (a raw/unscaled offset applied to the icons themselves, fixed
   in NewUIMainFrameWindow.cpp) compounded this the first time these numbers were set; both are
   fixed now. Every individual slot below (#item_slot_0..3, #skill_slot_0..4,
   #current_skill_slot) is positioned relative to ITS OWN wrapper here (both are `position:
   absolute`, so they're each other's containing block), so shifting just these two wrapper rules
   moves every child slot with them -- no need to touch each slot's own left value individually. */
#item_slots { position: absolute; left: 140px; top: 0; }
#skill_slots { position: absolute; left: 78px; top: 0; }

/* CNewUIItemHotKey::RenderItems() draws each 20x20 icon at x=10+i*38, y=443 -- padded 4px on
   every side here (not a guess: matches the visual weight of the gauge-slot/frame-btn borders
   above at this same reference scale). */
#item_slot_0 { position: absolute; left: 6px;   top: 439px; width: 28px; height: 28px; }
#item_slot_1 { position: absolute; left: 44px;  top: 439px; width: 28px; height: 28px; }
#item_slot_2 { position: absolute; left: 82px;  top: 439px; width: 28px; height: 28px; }
#item_slot_3 { position: absolute; left: 120px; top: 439px; width: 28px; height: 28px; }

/* Q/W/E/R hotkey label, centered above each slot, outside its box (see main_frame.rml's own
   comment) -- same bold+outline legibility treatment as .btn-icon-label/.gauge-num, just smaller.
   2026-09-01: was inside the box (top:1px/left:2px, upper-left corner) -- moved outside/above
   instead (feedback: "instead of putting them inside the potion's box, let's put them outside --
   at the top of the box, outside and centered"). -8px clears the slot's own 1px border with a
   couple px of breathing room above it (8px font, no descender to worry about). */
.slot-hotkey-label {
	position: absolute;
	left: 50%;
	top: -8px;
	transform: translateX(-50%);
	color: #ffffff;
	font-weight: bold;
	font-effect: outline(1px #000000);
	font-size: 8px;
	line-height: 8px;
	white-space: nowrap;
	pointer-events: none;
}

/* CNewUISkillList::RenderCurrentSkillAndHotSkillList()'s own 5-slot loop: x starts at 190, adds
   width(32) before each use, so slots land at 222/254/286/318/350, each exactly 32x38 -- unlike
   the item slots above, these 5 boxes are contiguous (pitch == width, no gap between them, same
   as IMAGE_SKILLBOX_USE's own legacy highlight box), so no padding is added here: an outward pad
   would make neighboring frames overlap by 2x the pad instead of sitting flush, doubling up the
   border where two slots touch. Exact native bounds instead -- adjacent frames share a single
   border line at the touching edge. */
#skill_slot_0 { position: absolute; left: 222px; top: 431px; width: 32px; height: 38px; }
#skill_slot_1 { position: absolute; left: 254px; top: 431px; width: 32px; height: 38px; }
#skill_slot_2 { position: absolute; left: 286px; top: 431px; width: 32px; height: 38px; }
#skill_slot_3 { position: absolute; left: 318px; top: 431px; width: 32px; height: 38px; }
#skill_slot_4 { position: absolute; left: 350px; top: 431px; width: 32px; height: 38px; }
/* Square off the touching inner edges (only the strip's outer corners stay rounded) -- with
   .slot-frame's border-radius left as-is on all 5, each touching seam would show two small
   rounded notches meeting instead of a flush line. Reads as one continuous 5-slot dock strip,
   the "unified styling" this whole redesign is going for. */
#skill_slot_0 { border-radius: 3px 0 0 3px; }
#skill_slot_1, #skill_slot_2, #skill_slot_3 { border-radius: 0; }
#skill_slot_4 { border-radius: 0 3px 3px 0; }
/* Selected-skill highlight (see main_frame.rml's own comment + CNewUISkillList::
   IsHotKeySlotCurrentSkill()) -- replaces the legacy IMAGE_SKILLBOX_USE sprite this function used
   to always draw for modern theme with a plain programmatic outline: brighter border + a subtle
   warm fill layered over .slot-frame/.slot-fill's own base values (ordinary CSS specificity, a
   class+class pair beats a lone class, no !important needed -- same convention as
   .frame-btn:hover/.open above). 2026-09-01: fill alpha was 60 -- feedback liked the border as-is
   but wanted the fill more transparent, so it reads as a highlight rather than a flat color swap;
   dropped to 25. */
#skill_slots .selected {
	border: 1px #e0c060;
	background-color: rgba(224, 192, 96, 25);
}
/* Same function's currently-selected-skill icon: x=392, y=437, 20x28 -- padded 4px like the
   potion slots (this one has no legacy highlight box of its own to match, it's the lone icon
   sitting directly in the center-band chrome). */
#current_skill_slot { position: absolute; left: 388px; top: 433px; width: 28px; height: 36px; }
